# Vendored upstream, cloned by setup rather than committed. See the README.
sdk/
littlefs/
# The bare name too, on ITS OWN line: git has no inline comments, so
# "littlefs   # note" is a pattern for a file literally called that plus spaces,
# and it silently stops matching. A symlink named littlefs is not caught by
# "littlefs/" (that matches a directory), and one committed from a worktree
# replaced the vendored checkout on a merge once. This line prevents the repeat.
littlefs

# Build directories
build/
Build/
build-*/
os/build/
os/build-*/
os/build_*/
loader-spike/build/
loader-spike/build-*/

# Built images. build.sh regenerates these, and at ~880 KB each they would add a
# megabyte to history on every build. Release artifacts belong on a release, not
# in the tree.
out/

# CMake generated files
CMakeFiles/
CMakeCache.txt
cmake_install.cmake
Makefile
install_manifest.txt
compile_commands.json

# Compiled object files
#
# NOTE: no "*.app" rule here, deliberately. A .app is an RPCortex PACKAGE — a
# relocatable object file the device loads and runs at runtime — not a macOS
# bundle or a stray executable. Ignoring it would silently drop the one artifact
# this project exists to produce.
*.d
*.o
*.obj
*.slo
*.lo
*.gch
*.pch
*.ilk
*.pdb
*.dwo

# Compiled libraries
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.dll
*.a
*.la
*.lai
*.lib

# Executables
*.exe

# Temporary files
*.tmp
*.log
*.bak
*.swp

# Test output and cache
Testing/
.cache/

# Working notes, kept in the workspace rather than the repository.
#
# STATUS.md and VS-V1.md tracked what was proven and what was not, week by week,
# and STRESS-CRASH.md was one bug's evidence. Useful while building; not what
# somebody cloning this wants to read, and written in a voice meant for whoever
# was in the middle of it. The engineering documents that ARE for a reader —
# ARCHITECTURE.md, TESTING.md, and the two design notes under os/ — stay.
#
# Listed as well as removed, so they cannot wander back in.
STATUS.md
VS-V1.md
os/STRESS-CRASH.md
CONTAIN-RESUME.md
os/CONTAIN-RESUME.md

# The emulator platform is a third-party checkout plus a built DLL; emu/run.sh
# fetches and builds it on first use.
emu/Renode_RP2040/

# Worktrees this session creates for parallel work. Never part of the tree.
.claude/
